WEBVTT

1
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:56,360
Erika!

2
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:01,880
Bring the pigs into the barn!

3
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,320
Erika, the pigs!

4
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:36,120
Bring the pigs into the barn!

5
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:44,160
The coast is clear.

6
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:56,480
Berta?

7
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:13,280
You rascals!

8
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:24,560
Just you wait!

9
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:01,120
Hurry up, everyone's arrived.

10
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:02,400
For me?

11
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:04,480
Mother picked it out for you.

12
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:06,000
For All Souls' Day.

13
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:08,920
Come on, get dressed.

14
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:34,960
What should I do?

15
00:12:35,280 --> 00:12:37,360
Watch what the others do.

16
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:11,360
<i>Holy eternal God</i>

17
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:15,840
<i>Today,
we remember the people</i>

18
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:20,120
<i>You have called from our midst</i>

19
00:15:22,040 --> 00:15:24,080
<i>And also remember</i>

20
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:28,600
<i>That we, too,
must die one day</i>

21
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:39,040
Mother is gagging again.
Her stomach does whatever it wants.

22
00:16:39,760 --> 00:16:41,680
Everyone hears it in the silence,

23
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:45,080
but pretends not to notice.

24
00:16:52,280 --> 00:16:55,240
Blinking once means:
I love you.

25
00:16:55,320 --> 00:16:58,600
Blinking twice means:
I love you very much.

26
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:04,000
We keep a tally of
who gets the most blinks from Mother.

27
00:17:07,800 --> 00:17:10,119
Hedda is usually ahead.

28
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:12,839
But only because
she's our cripple.

29
00:17:27,079 --> 00:17:29,120
<i>Thy kingdom come</i>

30
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:32,960
<i>Thy will be done
on Earth as it is in Heaven</i>

31
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:36,680
<i>Give us this day
our daily bread</i>

32
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:39,320
<i>And forgive us our trespasses</i>

33
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:43,400
<i>As we forgive those
who trespass against us</i>

34
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:46,440
<i>And lead us not into temptation</i>

35
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:50,320
<i>But deliver us from evil,
for Thine...</i>

36
00:19:58,680 --> 00:19:59,680
And?

37
00:20:00,280 --> 00:20:03,920
Do you think next year
your picture will be on the dresser?

38
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:07,840
I'm not afraid.

39
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:11,400
- You don't need to fear death.
- No.

40
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:13,520
I'm not.

41
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:16,640
But I'm not getting in a box,
I tell you.

42
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:21,600
Nobody knows

43
00:20:22,840 --> 00:20:26,400
if you notice
anything they do to you.

44
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:30,600
Nobody can say

45
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:34,720
if you don't notice anymore.
What they do to you.

46
00:20:39,960 --> 00:20:41,840
I've seen them all die.

47
00:20:44,720 --> 00:20:46,480
I've survived them all.

48
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:50,200
Your mother, too.

49
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:57,280
- Yes, my Fanni.
- I know, I know.

50
00:21:23,520 --> 00:21:24,760
Bye.

51
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:51,800
Put a potato sack
over Trudi's head

52
00:21:52,120 --> 00:21:54,240
and do it
for your country.

53
00:23:53,240 --> 00:23:54,800
Looks like Mother.

54
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:00,360
It is Mother.

55
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:20,400
Who's the girl?

56
00:24:22,600 --> 00:24:23,800
Alma.

57
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:26,280
That's not true.

58
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:29,600
It is.

59
00:24:30,480 --> 00:24:31,800
It's Alma.

60
00:24:34,920 --> 00:24:36,720
But I'm Alma.

61
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:40,840
She looks like you.

62
00:24:43,640 --> 00:24:46,160
Maybe her spirit
passed into you.

63
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:49,360
Perhaps you're not yourself,
but her.

64
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:57,720
I think she's just asleep.
She doesn't look like she's dead.

65
00:24:59,320 --> 00:25:03,280
Maybe they thought she was dead
and buried her alive.

66
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,680
She stole the sugar beet
from the cellar

67
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:09,560
and is calmly eating it
in her coffin.

68
00:25:17,560 --> 00:25:19,720
How old was she
when she died?

69
00:25:23,120 --> 00:25:24,440
Seven.

70
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:29,400
Why did she die so early?

71
00:25:33,280 --> 00:25:34,440
I don't know.

72
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:38,840
She was ailing.

73
00:25:42,120 --> 00:25:43,360
At some point,

74
00:25:45,800 --> 00:25:47,880
she just didn't wake up
anymore.

75
00:26:34,520 --> 00:26:35,600
Lia?

76
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:17,160
What did you mean by
she just didn't wake up?

77
00:27:17,240 --> 00:27:18,720
Sleep, Alma.

78
00:27:40,720 --> 00:27:42,880
What happens
when you're dead?

79
00:27:46,080 --> 00:27:47,200
Nothing.

80
00:28:54,760 --> 00:28:56,240
It was a dream.

81
00:30:38,600 --> 00:30:39,760
Mama?

82
00:30:40,680 --> 00:30:41,680
Yes?

83
00:30:42,560 --> 00:30:45,600
- Know what I think is funny?
- What?

84
00:30:46,920 --> 00:30:49,040
When I see a door handle,

85
00:30:49,400 --> 00:30:52,000
I know exactly
what it tastes like,

86
00:30:52,480 --> 00:30:55,360
though I've never licked one.

87
00:30:55,920 --> 00:30:57,480
Isn't that funny?

88
00:31:01,760 --> 00:31:05,960
Probably because you always put
everything in your mouth as a baby.

89
00:31:07,880 --> 00:31:11,120
Cigarettes from the ground,
dog poop...

90
00:31:12,360 --> 00:31:14,440
- That's not true!
- Yes, it is.

91
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:27,440
But how did I reach the door handle
as a baby?

92
00:31:27,520 --> 00:31:29,160
Time to sleep.

93
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,080
Have you ordered
the dumpsters yet?

94
00:31:45,240 --> 00:31:46,360
Shit.

95
00:31:48,880 --> 00:31:50,760
I forgot to call back.

96
00:31:54,080 --> 00:31:57,600
Order two small ones
instead of a large one this time.

97
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:06,720
And tell them to put them
directly under the windows,

98
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:09,080
so I can dump the rubble out
with Lenka.

99
00:32:18,600 --> 00:32:19,480
Who was that?

100
00:32:23,080 --> 00:32:24,440
This girl,

101
00:32:25,760 --> 00:32:27,440
a few houses away.

102
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:30,160
The daughter of the...

103
00:32:31,240 --> 00:32:32,320
one...

104
00:32:34,240 --> 00:32:35,480
whose mother died.

105
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:04,200
Warm.

106
00:36:39,960 --> 00:36:41,920
Nelly, move,
it's heavy.

107
00:36:49,200 --> 00:36:50,680
Wait, like this.

108
00:37:13,920 --> 00:37:14,920
Gotcha!

109
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:39,000
Now cover your right eye.

110
00:38:39,440 --> 00:38:40,760
- Like this?
- Exactly.

111
00:38:40,840 --> 00:38:43,640
And look at the X
with your left eye.

112
00:38:44,960 --> 00:38:47,480
Exactly,
now move slowly toward it.

113
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:55,000
When the O disappears,
you've found your blind spot.

114
00:38:58,680 --> 00:39:02,120
- Crazy, right?
- Yeah, it was gone, unbelievable.

115
00:39:02,200 --> 00:39:06,040
- Simply gone?
- That's literally your blind spot.

116
00:39:06,640 --> 00:39:08,960
- The optician said so.
- You wanna try?

117
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:12,600
- That's quite something.
- How? On the left?

118
00:39:12,680 --> 00:39:16,800
Keep that eye closed,
and then... Wait.

119
00:39:16,880 --> 00:39:20,120
- Like this?
- Show me the glasses you chose.

120
00:39:21,040 --> 00:39:22,960
For me,
it doesn't disappear.

121
00:39:23,320 --> 00:39:26,600
Because you're doing it wrong.
I'll explain it to you later.

122
00:39:27,600 --> 00:39:28,720
They're chic.

123
00:39:30,080 --> 00:39:32,840
It's quite something
when you consider

124
00:39:33,440 --> 00:39:35,920
that the world
is actually upside down.

125
00:39:37,960 --> 00:39:39,520
The optician said so.

126
00:39:40,720 --> 00:39:43,120
The brain mirrors everything

127
00:39:43,440 --> 00:39:45,320
before it reaches
our noggin.

128
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:49,200
But is it like that
for everyone?

129
00:39:49,760 --> 00:39:51,520
Everyone except you.

130
00:39:54,080 --> 00:39:55,200
Come on.

131
00:39:56,280 --> 00:39:57,600
Give it to me.

132
00:39:58,560 --> 00:40:00,680
- What are you doing?
- Go ahead.

133
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:06,960
Don't make such a face.
There are worse things.

134
00:40:10,960 --> 00:40:13,480
Did you thank Uncle Uwe
for the ride into town?

135
00:40:13,560 --> 00:40:15,920
No need to,
I was happy to do it.

136
00:40:17,880 --> 00:40:19,440
What, sit down.

137
00:40:32,400 --> 00:40:33,600
What now?

138
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:39,360
Did you get the glasses?
Let me see.

139
00:40:39,440 --> 00:40:41,120
They look chic.

140
00:40:42,520 --> 00:40:44,200
Did you make sugar cake?

141
00:40:44,800 --> 00:40:47,000
- Give me a slice, please.
- No, come in.

142
00:40:47,080 --> 00:40:48,240
Angelika...

143
00:40:50,320 --> 00:40:51,360
Say, Uwe,

144
00:40:52,520 --> 00:40:55,400
I can't wrap my head
around the hydraulics.

145
00:40:55,480 --> 00:40:56,920
What's wrong this time?

146
00:40:57,000 --> 00:40:58,760
- Can you look at it?
- Sure.

147
00:40:58,840 --> 00:41:00,840
You're getting crumbs all over.

148
00:41:08,600 --> 00:41:10,600
Hold the stroke
down to your hips.

149
00:41:14,120 --> 00:41:15,520
Rainer, let's go!

150
00:41:17,280 --> 00:41:18,920
You'll rest in a minute.

151
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:41,000
Two more weeks.

152
00:41:43,680 --> 00:41:44,920
I'll manage it.

153
00:41:45,440 --> 00:41:46,880
You have to manage it.

154
00:41:54,840 --> 00:41:56,560
The others don't sleep, either.

155
00:41:59,560 --> 00:42:02,240
With a bit more tension,
I'll be fine.

156
00:42:05,800 --> 00:42:07,760
Then get it from somewhere.

157
00:42:11,440 --> 00:42:13,640
Practice the motions
every day.

158
00:42:17,040 --> 00:42:19,640
You have time now
to go in once a day.

159
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:24,840
Excellent.

160
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:28,480
But you're still using
too much energy.

161
00:42:31,120 --> 00:42:33,920
Nice long strokes,
and follow through.

162
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:54,680
You can't keep up that pace
for the full distance.

163
00:43:02,560 --> 00:43:05,360
You guys need to keep
the movements in mind.

164
00:43:06,720 --> 00:43:10,480
Go through the movements
every night before you go to bed.

165
00:43:10,880 --> 00:43:14,200
I often pretended I didn't notice
how they looked at me.

166
00:43:17,000 --> 00:43:19,640
As if I were
immersed in thought.

167
00:43:21,960 --> 00:43:24,000
But it was actually me

168
00:43:24,080 --> 00:43:28,040
who was secretly watching them
looking at me.

169
00:46:23,080 --> 00:46:26,920
Mama, look.
I can even do it with one arm. Look!

170
00:46:33,080 --> 00:46:35,280
Mama,
you weren't even looking.

171
00:46:35,640 --> 00:46:37,640
I did, sweetie,
it was great!

172
00:46:37,720 --> 00:46:39,800
That's not true,
you weren't looking.

173
00:46:39,880 --> 00:46:42,560
How would you know?
You were underwater.

174
00:46:42,640 --> 00:46:44,120
Come on,
show me again.

175
00:46:51,680 --> 00:46:52,800
Great!

176
00:47:00,120 --> 00:47:01,120
Look!

177
00:47:02,880 --> 00:47:06,200
<i>And one and two
and three and four</i>

178
00:47:06,680 --> 00:47:10,600
<i>A hat, a stick,
an umbrella</i>

179
00:47:10,680 --> 00:47:14,360
<i>And forwards, backwards, to the side,
and one and two...</i>

180
00:50:29,880 --> 00:50:31,040
Nelly?

181
00:50:35,400 --> 00:50:36,520
Nelly!

182
00:51:15,000 --> 00:51:18,000
I love it when the skin smells
musty like a river.

183
00:51:19,960 --> 00:51:21,320
And the smell of cellars.

184
00:51:22,600 --> 00:51:26,120
I love the smell of cellars.
I'm addicted to it.

185
00:51:27,440 --> 00:51:30,680
- And nail polish.
- Yeah, but not as much as cellars.

186
00:51:32,400 --> 00:51:34,160
First,
the smell of cellars.

187
00:51:34,240 --> 00:51:35,200
Papa's coming.

188
00:51:35,280 --> 00:51:38,520
Then river smell on my skin
and then nail polish.

189
00:51:42,280 --> 00:51:44,280
Damn, it's so nice here.

190
00:51:45,760 --> 00:51:47,480
Isn't it nice here,
children?

191
00:51:47,560 --> 00:51:48,600
Yes!

192
00:51:49,680 --> 00:51:53,040
Now I can do an underwater handstand
with one arm.

193
00:51:53,120 --> 00:51:54,680
Yeah, really cool.

194
00:51:54,760 --> 00:51:56,440
And I found black sand.

195
00:51:57,880 --> 00:51:59,240
Muddy sand!

196
00:51:59,680 --> 00:52:00,800
Are you hungry?

197
00:52:00,880 --> 00:52:04,440
We have bread, cheese,
sausage and wine here.

198
00:52:06,360 --> 00:52:08,480
I saw a grasshopper.

199
00:52:08,560 --> 00:52:11,000
Yeah, a big green grasshopper.

200
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:13,240
What are you doing?

201
00:52:13,720 --> 00:52:15,440
Always up to some nonsense.

202
00:52:29,640 --> 00:52:32,600
Too bad you never know
when you're at your happiest.

203
00:52:41,680 --> 00:52:43,560
Eight, nine, ten.

204
00:52:46,840 --> 00:52:48,880
One, two, three...

205
00:52:50,040 --> 00:52:51,720
That one counts
three times.

206
00:52:52,040 --> 00:52:53,080
At least.

207
00:52:54,080 --> 00:52:58,400
Seven, eight, nine, ten.

208
00:53:01,000 --> 00:53:02,040
Eleven.

209
00:53:03,120 --> 00:53:04,320
I won.

210
00:53:13,840 --> 00:53:15,000
Warm.

211
00:53:42,200 --> 00:53:43,360
Does it hurt?

212
00:53:45,160 --> 00:53:46,440
Nonsense.

213
00:53:51,360 --> 00:53:53,720
First one to the oak tree.

214
00:55:26,440 --> 00:55:27,880
Tie a knot.

215
00:55:56,520 --> 00:55:59,200
Uwe, Uwe...

216
00:56:01,040 --> 00:56:03,120
I'll show you how it's done!

217
00:56:06,200 --> 00:56:07,800
That doesn't count!

218
00:56:08,400 --> 00:56:11,160
This isn't my bike!
I'll do it again!

219
00:56:13,720 --> 00:56:15,200
Now I can do it.

220
00:56:15,600 --> 00:56:17,000
Now it counts.

221
00:56:21,320 --> 00:56:22,400
I got it!

222
00:56:23,480 --> 00:56:24,560
Yes!

223
00:56:26,400 --> 00:56:27,920
Into the tub!

224
00:56:31,800 --> 00:56:33,400
Volker, Volker...

225
00:56:34,440 --> 00:56:35,720
You can't do it!

226
00:56:40,280 --> 00:56:41,840
Now it's Irm's turn!

227
00:56:45,640 --> 00:56:47,320
Irm, Irm...

228
00:56:47,400 --> 00:56:48,600
You can do it.

229
00:57:01,520 --> 00:57:02,600
Come on!

230
00:57:10,680 --> 00:57:14,480
- I'm unlucky at such things.
- It's got nothing to do with luck.

231
00:57:18,920 --> 00:57:21,040
Come on,
someone else do it.

232
00:57:23,640 --> 00:57:26,120
- Try it again.
- I'll do it.

233
00:57:29,120 --> 00:57:30,400
My turn!

234
00:57:38,320 --> 00:57:41,400
Angelika, Angelika...

235
00:58:21,360 --> 00:58:22,360
Now?

236
00:58:28,200 --> 00:58:29,200
Now?

237
00:58:34,720 --> 00:58:35,840
Now!

238
00:58:39,680 --> 00:58:40,680
Now.

239
00:59:17,040 --> 00:59:18,680
You've got a boner.

240
00:59:21,640 --> 00:59:22,720
No, I don't.

241
00:59:22,800 --> 00:59:24,920
- Get up.
- Let's see.

242
00:59:25,000 --> 00:59:26,720
Better not.

243
00:59:26,800 --> 00:59:27,920
Stand up.

244
00:59:28,000 --> 00:59:30,200
- Disgusting.
- Get naked!

245
00:59:31,400 --> 00:59:32,680
Get up.

246
00:59:33,160 --> 00:59:36,000
Pull down your pants.
Angelika, pull down his pants.

247
00:59:36,080 --> 00:59:38,120
Not much to see,
it's so small.

248
00:59:40,640 --> 00:59:42,040
Come on, Rainer, get up.

249
00:59:42,120 --> 00:59:44,040
Get up, get up...

250
00:59:44,120 --> 00:59:46,200
Pull down your pants!
Take them off!

251
00:59:59,800 --> 01:00:00,880
Take it easy.

252
01:00:13,760 --> 01:00:14,760
Just here.

253
01:00:38,840 --> 01:00:40,640
Will you drive me
to Gl?wisch today?

254
01:00:45,040 --> 01:00:46,040
Gl?wisch?

255
01:00:50,320 --> 01:00:51,880
What are you doing
in Gl?wisch?

256
01:00:54,360 --> 01:00:55,640
Disco?

257
01:01:23,000 --> 01:01:24,360
Maybe.

258
01:01:30,080 --> 01:01:31,840
Where were you earlier?

259
01:02:15,000 --> 01:02:17,200
Drinking schnapps
with the others.

260
01:02:43,000 --> 01:02:45,320
Will you drive me
to Gl?wisch now?

261
01:02:55,200 --> 01:02:56,240
Sorry.

262
01:02:57,120 --> 01:02:58,720
I have other plans.

263
01:03:09,120 --> 01:03:11,160
How will you get
to Gl?wisch now?

264
01:03:28,840 --> 01:03:30,560
Come on, go home!

265
01:04:36,120 --> 01:04:38,360
Today was good.
Everything worked out.

266
01:04:47,920 --> 01:04:49,560
Everyone had fun.

267
01:04:52,760 --> 01:04:54,520
There was enough food.

268
01:05:00,120 --> 01:05:02,760
Haven't laughed
that much in a long time.

269
01:05:04,640 --> 01:05:07,040
We have leftover pasta salad.

270
01:05:13,680 --> 01:05:15,920
The children were funny, too.

271
01:05:17,640 --> 01:05:19,840
I've never seen Anna
laugh so much.

272
01:05:20,200 --> 01:05:22,120
She usually
just complains.

273
01:05:25,640 --> 01:05:27,880
Good thing
I baked another cake.

274
01:05:28,760 --> 01:05:30,800
Or it might've been
too little.

275
01:05:30,880 --> 01:05:34,200
I remember how Mama
never knew when to laugh.

276
01:05:36,360 --> 01:05:39,360
Whenever something was funny,
she never laughed.

277
01:05:41,840 --> 01:05:44,120
She laughed when
there was nothing to laugh at,

278
01:05:44,200 --> 01:05:46,680
when something bad happened.

279
01:05:46,760 --> 01:05:49,840
When someone died,
she suddenly grinned.

280
01:05:51,120 --> 01:05:54,120
She didn't want to,
her body did it all by itself.

281
01:05:56,000 --> 01:05:59,960
And the more she tried not to grin,
the more she grinned.

282
01:06:00,360 --> 01:06:02,520
Until she had
a real laughing fit.

283
01:06:04,320 --> 01:06:08,960
She covered her mouth with her hand
so no one could see and turned red.

284
01:06:12,040 --> 01:06:14,960
It's funny that you turn red
when you're ashamed.

285
01:06:15,480 --> 01:06:18,560
In your face,
so everyone can see it.

286
01:06:31,320 --> 01:06:34,480
We played a prank on Mom
for her birthday.

287
01:06:40,080 --> 01:06:44,080
It was Dad's idea.
He always wanted to make Mom laugh.

288
01:06:55,800 --> 01:06:57,360
It never worked.

289
01:07:27,320 --> 01:07:29,200
<i>Long may she live</i>

290
01:07:29,280 --> 01:07:31,240
<i>Long may she live</i>

291
01:07:31,320 --> 01:07:33,960
<i>Three cheers for her</i>

292
01:07:35,080 --> 01:07:36,960
<i>Long may she live</i>

293
01:07:37,040 --> 01:07:39,040
<i>Long may she live</i>

294
01:07:39,120 --> 01:07:41,560
<i>Three cheers for her</i>

295
01:07:42,640 --> 01:07:46,440
Hip, hip, hooray!

296
01:07:48,560 --> 01:07:50,280
All the best, Mom!

297
01:07:51,080 --> 01:07:52,960
<i>Let her grow old</i>

298
01:07:53,040 --> 01:07:55,440
<i>Three cheers for her</i>

299
01:09:48,800 --> 01:09:51,560
I'm glad you got
to know each other.

300
01:09:52,600 --> 01:09:54,800
Lenka doesn't know
anyone here yet.

301
01:09:55,560 --> 01:09:57,240
Nobody lives here.

302
01:10:06,320 --> 01:10:07,960
How's your papa?

303
01:10:09,240 --> 01:10:11,400
Good. Hanging in there.

304
01:10:18,360 --> 01:10:20,120
Can I sleep here?

305
01:10:23,360 --> 01:10:24,560
Well...

306
01:10:27,600 --> 01:10:30,200
Well, if you guys want her to.

307
01:10:32,800 --> 01:10:33,920
Today?

308
01:10:36,120 --> 01:10:38,280
We'll have to ask
your papa first.

309
01:10:43,120 --> 01:10:44,800
Nice cell phone.

310
01:10:45,680 --> 01:10:46,680
Purple.

311
01:10:47,680 --> 01:10:49,120
It was my mother's.

312
01:10:49,560 --> 01:10:50,880
I inherited it.

313
01:10:52,960 --> 01:10:54,280
Yeah, it's me.

314
01:10:55,280 --> 01:10:58,560
The mother of my new friend
wants to ask you something.

315
01:11:06,960 --> 01:11:09,440
Yeah, this is Christa
from house 10.

316
01:11:12,280 --> 01:11:13,600
Yes, from Berlin.

317
01:11:18,720 --> 01:11:21,200
So, your daughter
is here right now...

318
01:11:23,080 --> 01:11:24,200
Good night.

319
01:11:31,480 --> 01:11:33,920
- Good night.
- Sleep well.

320
01:11:36,240 --> 01:11:37,360
Sleep well.

321
01:11:50,760 --> 01:11:54,320
If anything's wrong, come wake us up.
No matter what it is.

322
01:12:16,760 --> 01:12:18,600
Could you maybe sing me
a lullaby?

323
01:12:23,040 --> 01:12:25,280
Did your mama
sing to you at night?

324
01:12:35,560 --> 01:12:37,080
I have to warn you,

325
01:12:37,680 --> 01:12:39,840
I'm not such a good singer.

326
01:12:40,560 --> 01:12:42,000
Doesn't matter.

327
01:12:52,080 --> 01:12:55,040
<i>Good evening, good night</i>

328
01:12:56,400 --> 01:12:59,600
<i>Covered with roses</i>

329
01:13:01,200 --> 01:13:03,840
<i>Adorned with cloves</i>

330
01:13:05,240 --> 01:13:08,120
<i>Slip under the covers</i>

331
01:13:09,520 --> 01:13:12,640
<i>Tomorrow morning, if God wills</i>

332
01:13:13,560 --> 01:13:16,480
<i>You'll wake once again</i>

333
01:13:17,920 --> 01:13:21,080
<i>Tomorrow morning, if God wills</i>

334
01:13:22,000 --> 01:13:25,080
<i>You'll wake once again</i>

335
01:14:49,520 --> 01:14:51,560
Alma, let it go.

336
01:15:15,120 --> 01:15:16,240
Alma, don't!

337
01:16:57,960 --> 01:16:59,680
Work accident.

338
01:17:00,800 --> 01:17:02,520
Work accident.

339
01:17:04,560 --> 01:17:06,280
Work accident.

340
01:17:07,680 --> 01:17:10,680
If you say a word
too many times,

341
01:17:11,120 --> 01:17:13,640
it loses its meaning.

342
01:17:14,720 --> 01:17:17,080
Fritz had a work accident.

343
01:17:18,200 --> 01:17:19,960
Work accident.

344
01:17:24,240 --> 01:17:27,280
Are they gonna take off
his other leg as well?

345
01:17:29,720 --> 01:17:31,840
They're phantom pains.

346
01:17:33,280 --> 01:17:35,440
The doctor said
this could happen.

347
01:17:38,200 --> 01:17:39,840
Now it stopped.

348
01:17:51,800 --> 01:17:52,800
No.

349
01:17:54,280 --> 01:17:57,760
He's silent because they put
a stick between his teeth.

350
01:18:00,760 --> 01:18:02,240
If you listen closely,

351
01:18:03,720 --> 01:18:05,360
you can still hear
his whining.

352
01:18:07,720 --> 01:18:09,120
Hear it?

353
01:20:30,440 --> 01:20:34,960
Funny how something can hurt
that's no longer there.

354
01:21:08,400 --> 01:21:09,400
Warm.

355
01:21:12,600 --> 01:21:14,120
What's wrong?

356
01:21:15,480 --> 01:21:16,480
Nothing.

357
01:21:17,720 --> 01:21:19,200
Go back to sleep.

358
01:21:21,920 --> 01:21:24,120
Were you at Uncle Fritz's again?

359
01:21:35,120 --> 01:21:39,000
Mom knows things
she shouldn't even remember,

360
01:21:39,080 --> 01:21:41,920
because she wasn't even there
when they happened.

361
01:21:42,600 --> 01:21:44,600
She remembers, for example,

362
01:21:44,680 --> 01:21:48,480
that her sister Erika
always stole Uncle Fritz's crutches

363
01:21:48,880 --> 01:21:51,920
to see if she could walk
with just one leg.

364
01:21:53,000 --> 01:21:56,800
But if you ask her where she was
when all these things happened,

365
01:21:57,200 --> 01:21:58,800
she doesn't remember.

366
01:21:59,720 --> 01:22:02,800
Maybe because you only ever
see others from the outside

367
01:22:03,280 --> 01:22:04,720
but never yourself.

368
01:22:32,720 --> 01:22:33,840
Weirdo.

369
01:23:04,520 --> 01:23:06,080
Did you go to Gl?wisch?

370
01:23:15,720 --> 01:23:17,320
How did you get back?

371
01:23:22,680 --> 01:23:24,080
Hitchhiking.

372
01:23:41,560 --> 01:23:43,040
And who drove you?

373
01:23:56,800 --> 01:23:58,120
Your father.

374
01:24:01,560 --> 01:24:03,360
Was he at the disco, too?

375
01:24:04,480 --> 01:24:05,520
No.

376
01:24:13,440 --> 01:24:14,600
But?

377
01:24:18,840 --> 01:24:21,480
He drove by
while I was leaving.

378
01:25:03,400 --> 01:25:04,480
Rainer!

379
01:25:07,880 --> 01:25:09,400
You're my cousin.

380
01:25:16,720 --> 01:25:19,760
But my father being your uncle
doesn't bother you?

381
01:25:26,360 --> 01:25:27,440
What?

382
01:25:31,480 --> 01:25:34,160
Everyone knows
you let him bang you.

383
01:30:22,640 --> 01:30:25,680
We always say,
your actions are what count.

384
01:30:25,760 --> 01:30:27,680
A person is what they do.

385
01:30:28,440 --> 01:30:29,880
I don't believe that.

386
01:30:31,760 --> 01:30:34,120
I think you are
where you actually are,

387
01:30:35,680 --> 01:30:37,560
while doing something.

388
01:31:05,240 --> 01:31:07,480
When I was with a friend,

389
01:31:07,920 --> 01:31:09,720
it didn't take long

390
01:31:09,800 --> 01:31:12,680
for everyone to say
I spoke just like them.

391
01:31:13,440 --> 01:31:17,520
I even had to adopt
things I didn't like:

392
01:31:18,440 --> 01:31:21,520
the high-pitched laugh,
the kind of emphasis...

393
01:31:21,840 --> 01:31:26,120
I didn't set out to do it.
My body somehow did it on its own.

394
01:31:27,400 --> 01:31:30,640
Dad sometimes said
that it was enough.

395
01:31:30,720 --> 01:31:33,240
I should
be myself again.

396
01:31:35,120 --> 01:31:36,720
But being myself again?

397
01:31:36,800 --> 01:31:39,640
My body couldn't manage that
on its own.

398
01:31:39,720 --> 01:31:41,840
Then I became dizzy

399
01:31:41,920 --> 01:31:45,600
because I was trying
to remember how I speak.

400
01:31:46,760 --> 01:31:49,120
So I said my name in succession

401
01:31:49,640 --> 01:31:51,800
until it seemed foreign to me.

402
01:31:53,400 --> 01:31:57,520
Then I imagined
seeing myself through Dad's eyes

403
01:31:57,600 --> 01:31:59,560
and played the person

404
01:32:00,760 --> 01:32:04,360
he must envision when he says
I should be myself again.

405
01:32:11,840 --> 01:32:15,480
How long can you act happy
without anyone noticing?

406
01:33:02,720 --> 01:33:03,840
Work accident.

407
01:34:44,800 --> 01:34:46,960
It's been like this for days.

408
01:36:01,320 --> 01:36:04,400
Suddenly,
Mother could no longer walk.

409
01:36:04,480 --> 01:36:07,200
The doctor couldn't explain it,
either.

410
01:36:07,280 --> 01:36:11,400
Said she was perfectly healthy
and shouldn't make a fuss.

411
01:36:11,800 --> 01:36:14,760
Mother did her best,
but it was no use.

412
01:36:15,400 --> 01:36:17,640
Her legs no longer obeyed her.

413
01:36:18,840 --> 01:36:20,760
The doctor prescribed bed rest.

414
01:36:23,000 --> 01:36:25,080
In the evenings,
we prayed to God

415
01:36:25,160 --> 01:36:28,040
that Mother would soon
be able to walk again.

416
01:40:13,440 --> 01:40:14,800
Alma, where are you?

417
01:40:15,400 --> 01:40:17,000
Come out, you've won!

418
01:40:23,520 --> 01:40:25,280
Alma, are you around here?

419
01:40:25,360 --> 01:40:27,120
Right here! I'm right here!

420
01:40:27,200 --> 01:40:29,200
Alma, that's not funny. Come out.

421
01:40:32,360 --> 01:40:35,040
Here, I'm in the tree, Lia!
Look up!

422
01:40:36,440 --> 01:40:37,880
She's vanished.

423
01:40:38,440 --> 01:40:40,240
Here I am! Here!

424
01:40:40,320 --> 01:40:41,560
Can you see her?

425
01:40:41,960 --> 01:40:43,800
No. Looks like she's gone.

426
01:40:43,880 --> 01:40:45,720
Here I am, Lia!

427
01:40:48,320 --> 01:40:49,800
I'm here!

428
01:40:51,600 --> 01:40:52,840
Help!

429
01:41:00,440 --> 01:41:02,600
Lia! Gerti!

430
01:41:04,040 --> 01:41:05,840
Here I am! Here!

431
01:41:07,560 --> 01:41:08,920
Here in the tree!

432
01:41:11,120 --> 01:41:12,600
I'm here!

433
01:41:20,400 --> 01:41:21,560
Lia!

434
01:42:03,480 --> 01:42:04,520
Alma?

435
01:42:23,680 --> 01:42:27,920
How you can tell
if you're alive or dead?

436
01:42:29,240 --> 01:42:33,480
I got such a high temperature,
they wanted to call the doctor.

437
01:42:33,560 --> 01:42:35,840
He wouldn't have known
anything anyway.

438
01:42:38,640 --> 01:42:41,800
Trudi put her hand
on my forehead,

439
01:42:42,200 --> 01:42:46,960
like she does to calm the cows
when they have a calf.

440
01:42:51,120 --> 01:42:53,800
She spent the whole night
by my side.

441
01:42:55,920 --> 01:42:58,280
Mother initially resisted

442
01:42:58,360 --> 01:43:02,600
when everyone said
Trudi should put a hand on her, too.

443
01:43:02,960 --> 01:43:05,360
But in the end,
she let it happen.

444
01:43:06,240 --> 01:43:09,800
I'm sure that's why
she was able to walk again soon.

445
01:43:10,640 --> 01:43:14,520
The thing with Fritz
was more complicated.

446
01:43:14,600 --> 01:43:18,680
Trudi couldn't just
conjure up his leg again.

447
01:43:18,760 --> 01:43:20,360
Hedda was sure

448
01:43:20,440 --> 01:43:23,200
that the dogs
had long since eaten it.

449
01:43:23,280 --> 01:43:26,200
But she was able
to ease the phantom pain.

450
01:43:26,520 --> 01:43:29,280
She put her hand
on Fritz's center.

451
01:43:29,360 --> 01:43:33,040
He calmed down
and moaned much more quietly.

452
01:44:43,240 --> 01:44:45,840
With fat Berta,
it was easy for the men.

453
01:44:45,920 --> 01:44:48,400
She couldn't have children
by nature.

454
01:44:49,200 --> 01:44:51,480
Trudi wasn't so lucky.

455
01:44:54,200 --> 01:44:58,200
She first had to be
'made safe for the men.'

456
01:44:59,120 --> 01:45:01,400
As a maid,
you had to be quick

457
01:45:01,480 --> 01:45:05,520
and hard-working and not miss work
because you had a bun in the oven.

458
01:45:07,200 --> 01:45:08,720
I don't know exactly

459
01:45:08,800 --> 01:45:12,920
how they made Trudi
become like fat Berta for good.

460
01:45:13,440 --> 01:45:17,080
But right after
Trudi arrived here,

461
01:45:17,800 --> 01:45:20,320
she had to go away
for a few days.

462
01:45:20,400 --> 01:45:22,600
It must've been then.

463
01:45:22,680 --> 01:45:24,520
When she came back,

464
01:45:24,880 --> 01:45:27,680
the male servants
lined up at her door at night.

465
01:45:28,400 --> 01:45:31,640
I don't know if they put
a potato sack over her.

466
01:45:35,280 --> 01:45:39,080
She wasn't any prettier
than before, thought Gerti.

467
01:45:39,160 --> 01:45:42,200
She looked as if
she'd been plopped down on earth

468
01:45:42,280 --> 01:45:44,480
and didn't know
what to do with herself.

469
01:45:48,640 --> 01:45:52,320
Trudi once said
Fritz's life was entirely in vain.

470
01:45:53,080 --> 01:45:55,800
I think Trudi also lived in vain.

471
01:45:57,040 --> 01:45:59,480
Maybe that's why
she looked after him:

472
01:46:00,760 --> 01:46:04,560
so that it wasn't so lonely,
living in vain.

473
01:47:02,360 --> 01:47:03,520
And?

474
01:47:05,040 --> 01:47:06,440
I don't know.

475
01:47:07,720 --> 01:47:08,800
Let me try.

476
01:47:13,680 --> 01:47:15,600
And? Is the fly still inside?

477
01:47:17,920 --> 01:47:19,320
I hear something.

478
01:47:25,280 --> 01:47:27,960
The last one will be dragged
into the realm of the dead!

479
01:47:39,560 --> 01:47:43,120
The last one will be dragged
into the realm of the dead!

480
01:47:43,200 --> 01:47:46,200
The last one will be dragged
into the realm of the dead!

481
01:48:10,200 --> 01:48:14,760
Alma had been waiting all summer
to drop dead or not wake up.

482
01:48:15,120 --> 01:48:17,360
But somehow
God must've forgotten her.

483
01:48:22,840 --> 01:48:24,360
Instead, he took me.

484
01:48:36,400 --> 01:48:40,080
I might still be alive if Alma
had been the last one through.

485
01:48:46,400 --> 01:48:49,560
Alma still wasn't dead
by summer's end.

486
01:48:50,040 --> 01:48:51,840
But our great-grandmother.

487
01:48:52,240 --> 01:48:54,560
And then suddenly, me too.

488
01:48:55,200 --> 01:48:56,760
Don't cry.

489
01:48:57,800 --> 01:48:59,360
It wasn't for real.

490
01:49:14,520 --> 01:49:18,680
Alma thought great-grandmother
was the only person who wouldn't die.

491
01:49:18,760 --> 01:49:21,160
As if dying wouldn't suit her,

492
01:49:21,240 --> 01:49:23,360
because she was so afraid of it.

493
01:49:27,600 --> 01:49:30,120
Then her time had come.

494
01:49:38,000 --> 01:49:40,840
She simply toppled over one night

495
01:49:40,920 --> 01:49:42,920
on her way to the outhouse.

496
01:49:44,400 --> 01:49:46,880
She must've had
urgent business to attend to.

497
01:49:47,560 --> 01:49:50,320
Hedda laughed and said
she must've been scared shitless

498
01:49:50,400 --> 01:49:52,840
when she realized
the end was near.

499
01:49:54,840 --> 01:49:58,040
She had already
pulled up her robe

500
01:49:58,120 --> 01:50:00,600
so it would go quicker
when she got there.

501
01:50:01,120 --> 01:50:03,400
But she never arrived.

502
01:50:05,320 --> 01:50:09,280
Father said
it was the only time

503
01:50:09,600 --> 01:50:12,240
he had ever seen
his grandmother's bare bottom.

504
01:50:13,720 --> 01:50:16,400
And just like that,
it was the same with her

505
01:50:16,480 --> 01:50:19,080
as with the other people
who died.

506
01:50:34,800 --> 01:50:38,360
The window was opened
to let the soul out.

507
01:50:44,720 --> 01:50:46,800
The clocks were stopped.

508
01:50:51,280 --> 01:50:53,920
Stones against the evil eye.

509
01:50:56,440 --> 01:50:59,440
HERE I LIE AND MUST DECAY

510
01:51:04,040 --> 01:51:06,680
So that no flies crawl in.

511
01:51:18,760 --> 01:51:22,480
So the good Lord believes
she was a believer.

512
01:51:31,920 --> 01:51:34,960
Twice a day,
the village's woman of the dead came

513
01:51:35,040 --> 01:51:36,920
to wash the body.

514
01:51:37,480 --> 01:51:39,600
But because
our woman of the dead

515
01:51:39,680 --> 01:51:43,600
had passed away
two weeks earlier in childbirth,

516
01:51:43,680 --> 01:51:45,480
Trudi had to take over.

517
01:51:47,920 --> 01:51:52,280
Vinegar rags and nettles
are placed on the face of the dead.

518
01:51:59,200 --> 01:52:02,200
I remember
that at the end of the wake,

519
01:52:02,280 --> 01:52:05,200
her hands were
full of welts.

520
01:52:16,760 --> 01:52:19,160
<i>And lead us not into temptation</i>

521
01:52:19,240 --> 01:52:21,840
<i>But deliver us from evil</i>

522
01:52:21,920 --> 01:52:26,000
<i>For Thine is the kingdom,
and the power, and the glory...</i>

523
01:52:26,080 --> 01:52:27,520
We pray

524
01:52:28,400 --> 01:52:32,560
that she won't have
to meet her mother again.

525
01:52:37,440 --> 01:52:41,360
Whenever I try
to imagine her face,

526
01:52:42,200 --> 01:52:44,520
it's all a blur.

527
01:52:45,280 --> 01:52:47,880
As if I hadn't been able
to memorize it,

528
01:52:48,440 --> 01:52:50,680
though I saw her so often.

529
01:52:55,120 --> 01:52:59,440
But I still remember exactly
what her hands looked like.

530
01:53:10,440 --> 01:53:13,760
And the expression
when she was dead.

531
01:53:16,960 --> 01:53:20,480
Maybe because she never
looked like that in her life.

532
01:53:27,720 --> 01:53:29,760
She looked somehow astonished.

533
01:53:30,080 --> 01:53:34,760
Trudi said she looked like that
because she realized she was dying.

534
01:53:35,360 --> 01:53:37,000
But to me, it looked like

535
01:53:37,360 --> 01:53:41,600
she was seeing something
she had never expected.

536
01:53:43,000 --> 01:53:47,400
Hedda thinks
she may be reborn in Africa.

537
01:53:47,480 --> 01:53:50,040
Father said, then as a fly.

538
01:53:50,120 --> 01:53:53,080
At least he never killed another one.

539
01:53:55,680 --> 01:53:57,000
Warm.

540
01:54:49,440 --> 01:54:53,640
Mom never talked about
how her sister Erika died.

541
01:54:54,320 --> 01:54:56,200
I knew it anyway.

542
01:54:57,320 --> 01:54:59,480
Mom's uncle once told me.

543
01:55:00,040 --> 01:55:02,040
I got to meet him once.

544
01:55:02,120 --> 01:55:04,200
He visited us on the farm:

545
01:55:05,040 --> 01:55:07,160
Uncle Fritz
with his wooden leg.

546
01:55:07,520 --> 01:55:10,440
Told me to knock on it.
Brings good luck, he said.

547
01:55:32,040 --> 01:55:34,640
ATTENTION:
INTRA-GERMAN BORDER

548
01:55:55,520 --> 01:55:57,760
Erika went into the river,

549
01:55:57,840 --> 01:56:00,480
like many women
when the war was over.

550
01:56:02,040 --> 01:56:05,320
They had heard what happened
to women in the other villages.

551
01:56:06,600 --> 01:56:10,040
They feared what might come
more than they feared death.

552
01:56:13,200 --> 01:56:16,200
Mom gave Erika
a pinky promise.

553
01:56:16,680 --> 01:56:18,480
But when she was underwater,

554
01:56:18,560 --> 01:56:21,600
the eels swam up
and nipped at her.

555
01:56:23,160 --> 01:56:26,560
She wasn't the only one
to come back out of the river.

556
01:56:27,000 --> 01:56:31,080
She was still ashamed
of not having gone all the way.

557
01:56:33,120 --> 01:56:37,320
Sometimes I think
Mom is still underwater with Erika.

558
01:56:59,520 --> 01:57:00,680
Lia!

559
01:57:05,880 --> 01:57:08,760
I remember Nelly
wanting to come with us.

560
01:57:11,400 --> 01:57:14,840
I didn't allow it
because I didn't want her there.

561
01:57:22,440 --> 01:57:26,160
I remember thinking
that my life was in vain,

562
01:57:27,160 --> 01:57:29,560
because I hadn't taken strawberry
like Kaya,

563
01:57:31,600 --> 01:57:33,120
but rather vanilla.

564
01:57:42,840 --> 01:57:44,920
I believe
it was that summer

565
01:57:45,000 --> 01:57:47,440
I began to think:
if I were someone else,

566
01:57:47,520 --> 01:57:49,560
I'd be happier.

567
01:57:50,640 --> 01:57:53,240
I was even jealous
of the misfortune of others.

568
01:57:54,000 --> 01:57:56,520
Even that Kaya's mother had died.

569
01:58:00,760 --> 01:58:03,440
I wished something like that
would happen to me

570
01:58:03,520 --> 01:58:05,280
so I could become like her.

571
01:58:11,160 --> 01:58:12,000
Today I think:

572
01:58:12,600 --> 01:58:16,000
If only I hadn't thought
at that moment that my life was over

573
01:58:16,080 --> 01:58:19,560
just because I took vanilla
and not strawberry.

574
01:58:31,400 --> 01:58:33,760
How did your mother actually die?

575
01:58:42,920 --> 01:58:44,320
Lung cancer.

576
01:58:55,200 --> 01:58:56,800
Why do you smoke, then?

577
01:58:59,280 --> 01:59:01,000
Everyone dies of something.

578
01:59:01,840 --> 01:59:02,960
You, too.

579
01:59:14,320 --> 01:59:15,840
Were you there

580
01:59:18,480 --> 01:59:20,120
when your mother died?

581
01:59:27,280 --> 01:59:28,840
Come on, let's go swimming.

582
01:59:35,440 --> 01:59:36,640
Come on!

583
01:59:44,840 --> 01:59:46,680
Maybe we'll drown!

584
02:02:12,400 --> 02:02:13,560
Kaya?

585
02:03:00,920 --> 02:03:04,040
If I want to lift my leg,
I can do it.

586
02:03:04,720 --> 02:03:07,640
And if I want to lift my arm,
I can lift it.

587
02:03:09,320 --> 02:03:12,720
But when I command my heart
to stand still,

588
02:03:13,080 --> 02:03:14,600
then it doesn't.

589
02:03:15,720 --> 02:03:17,400
It just keeps on beating.

590
02:03:22,920 --> 02:03:25,480
Maybe I'm not deciding
hard enough

591
02:03:27,280 --> 02:03:29,320
just in case it does obey.

592
02:03:32,480 --> 02:03:34,520
Would it stop beating now,

593
02:03:35,920 --> 02:03:38,480
if I really, really wanted it?

594
02:03:41,520 --> 02:03:42,520
Now.

595
02:03:50,000 --> 02:03:51,080
Now.

596
02:03:58,880 --> 02:03:59,880
Now.

597
02:04:13,400 --> 02:04:14,240
Now.

598
02:04:23,640 --> 02:04:26,440
When in reality
you see everything upside down,

599
02:04:26,760 --> 02:04:29,600
maybe in reality
everything is the other way around.

600
02:04:33,560 --> 02:04:35,840
Actually,
you can then simply decide

601
02:04:35,920 --> 02:04:39,600
that what's bad
is really good.

602
02:05:11,040 --> 02:05:14,360
After the solstice, Lia starts
working as a maid on my farm.

603
02:05:22,840 --> 02:05:24,040
Good.

604
02:05:24,800 --> 02:05:26,840
Then it's a done deal.

605
02:05:31,280 --> 02:05:33,920
You take care
of the women's business.

606
02:08:18,640 --> 02:08:19,960
I knew it:

607
02:08:20,520 --> 02:08:22,000
You're a chicken.

608
02:08:27,000 --> 02:08:30,720
- Come here!
- Angelika, come here! Rainer!

609
02:08:30,800 --> 02:08:33,600
There! Stand still, stand still!

610
02:08:33,680 --> 02:08:35,080
That's good.

611
02:08:36,640 --> 02:08:39,080
Yes, that's good. Closer.

612
02:08:39,160 --> 02:08:40,680
Angelika, to the right.

613
02:08:40,760 --> 02:08:44,040
- Let's get together.
- Rainer, suck in your gut.

614
02:08:44,560 --> 02:08:47,520
Now I'm curious
about the miracle machine.

615
02:08:48,600 --> 02:08:51,240
- The miracle machine.
- Unbelievable.

616
02:09:33,560 --> 02:09:35,640
What does the little birdie say?

617
02:09:50,680 --> 02:09:55,000
- And now you can see the photo?
- Madness. Now just shake it?

618
02:09:55,080 --> 02:09:57,320
- Shake, shake.
- Wait a minute.

619
02:09:57,400 --> 02:09:58,760
Now I'm curious.

620
02:09:58,840 --> 02:10:00,680
There's nothing to see yet.

621
02:10:05,360 --> 02:10:08,760
If anyone said that's possible,
we would've called them crazy.

622
02:10:09,680 --> 02:10:11,840
But I look fat on it.

623
02:10:19,040 --> 02:10:21,880
But Angelika looks like a ghost.

624
02:10:24,560 --> 02:10:26,000
She's gone again.

625
02:10:26,960 --> 02:10:31,080
- But I'm not that fat. Look.
- Of course you are.

626
02:10:31,160 --> 02:10:32,760
So, the camera is...

627
02:10:55,960 --> 02:10:57,880
We looked for her for weeks,

628
02:10:57,960 --> 02:10:59,640
but she was simply gone.

629
02:11:03,520 --> 02:11:07,760
At some point, they said
she had swum across to West Germany.

630
02:11:09,200 --> 02:11:12,080
Not a word was spoken
about her at home.

631
02:11:13,280 --> 02:11:14,960
I never saw her again.

632
02:11:16,760 --> 02:11:19,560
Sometimes I think
she was just in my head,

633
02:11:19,640 --> 02:11:21,440
and never really existed.

634
02:11:26,720 --> 02:11:27,960
Angelika.

635
02:11:53,920 --> 02:11:57,520
I remember taking off
my soaking wet T-shirt,

636
02:11:57,600 --> 02:12:00,040
because it was sticking
to my body so much.

637
02:12:02,120 --> 02:12:04,200
Only later did I realize

638
02:12:04,280 --> 02:12:07,440
I was the only one running around
topless and in panties.

639
02:12:12,720 --> 02:12:14,720
I remember
the smell of wet grass.

640
02:12:16,440 --> 02:12:17,840
And this look

641
02:12:19,000 --> 02:12:20,920
from my parents' friend.

642
02:12:22,000 --> 02:12:24,000
I can't even remember his name.

643
02:12:26,960 --> 02:12:30,000
I know he realized
I'd seen the look in his eyes.

644
02:12:32,280 --> 02:12:34,160
I can still feel myself blushing

645
02:12:34,800 --> 02:12:37,680
and would've loved
to cross my arms in front of me.

646
02:12:38,000 --> 02:12:39,920
But that would've been
too obvious.

647
02:12:42,840 --> 02:12:46,520
When I pretended
not to notice his gaze, it was

648
02:12:47,040 --> 02:12:49,320
as if I was sharing
a secret with him

649
02:12:49,400 --> 02:12:51,400
that I didn't want to share.

650
02:14:18,720 --> 02:14:19,720
Lia!

651
02:14:41,240 --> 02:14:43,640
It rained a lot that summer.

652
02:14:44,040 --> 02:14:46,280
We lost an entire harvest.

653
02:14:49,680 --> 02:14:51,400
The neighboring farmer

654
02:14:51,480 --> 02:14:54,400
reimbursed us
for half of the lost grain.

655
02:14:55,560 --> 02:14:58,360
In return,
he asked Lia to be his maid.

656
02:14:58,440 --> 02:15:02,480
After the solstice,
she left us and went to him.

657
02:15:04,840 --> 02:15:09,000
Before that, she had to see Elli,
just like Trudi back then.

658
02:15:09,920 --> 02:15:12,680
She couldn't start working
for him for three weeks

659
02:15:12,760 --> 02:15:14,960
because she almost
bled to death.

660
02:15:21,040 --> 02:15:25,440
Later, they asked me what had
happened to Lia in the field.

661
02:15:25,520 --> 02:15:28,480
I said: a work accident.

662
02:17:13,559 --> 02:17:16,000
50, 49...

663
02:18:08,360 --> 02:18:11,440
When I try to remember
that one summer,

664
02:18:12,240 --> 02:18:15,360
I always wonder
what Nelly saw last.

665
02:18:16,959 --> 02:18:19,840
And whether
I should've noticed anything.

666
02:18:24,200 --> 02:18:27,280
I think Nelly was playing "War"
with the other children.

667
02:18:27,879 --> 02:18:29,480
They sat by the fire,

668
02:18:29,920 --> 02:18:32,840
pretending to
eat rats and frogs' legs.

669
02:18:43,200 --> 02:18:45,840
But I only remember this girl

670
02:18:48,440 --> 02:18:50,799
that I met at the river.

671
02:18:54,480 --> 02:18:56,360
I think her name was Kaya.

672
02:19:11,600 --> 02:19:13,600
We listened to one song
all summer,

673
02:19:15,080 --> 02:19:17,120
her mother's favorite.

674
02:19:48,920 --> 02:19:50,400
Want a popsicle?

675
02:19:56,280 --> 02:19:57,920
Strawberry or vanilla?

676
02:19:58,000 --> 02:19:59,040
Strawberry.

677
02:19:59,880 --> 02:20:01,040
Here you go.

678
02:20:02,440 --> 02:20:05,080
- And you? Vanilla, as always?
- I'll take strawberry.

679
02:20:07,680 --> 02:20:08,880
Strawberry.

680
02:20:09,280 --> 02:20:10,440
Thanks.

681
02:20:12,360 --> 02:20:14,040
I want strawberry, too.

682
02:20:15,520 --> 02:20:18,520
Oh, man, sorry, sweetie.
Only vanilla's left.

683
02:20:19,200 --> 02:20:20,600
We can swap.

684
02:20:45,880 --> 02:20:46,760
You're it!

685
